[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62820,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyomatosis peritonealis disseminata is a condition characterized by the presence of multiple benign smooth muscle nodules on the peritoneal surface of pelvic and abdominal cavities.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62821,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8890/1 Leiomyomatosis, peritonealis disseminata</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62822,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F74 &amp; XH8MR2 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of peritoneum &amp; Leiomyomatosis, peritonealis disseminata</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62823,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> disseminated peritoneal leiomyomatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62824,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62825,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pelvic and abdominal peritoneum; uterine, adnexal, and intestinal surfaces; omentum</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62826,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyomatosis peritonealis disseminata is often an incidental finding at the time of caesarean section or postpartum tubal ligation. In some cases patients may have symptoms related to uterine leiomyomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62827,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients are of reproductive age or (rarely) postmenopausal. More than 70% are pregnant or using oral contraceptives, and exceptionally there is an underlying estrogen-producing ovarian neoplasm {8604458; 11426907; 1326849}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62828,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "<p>The etiology is presumed to involve metaplasia from submesothelial smooth muscle or mesenchymal cells, initiated or promoted by estrogen and progesterone {12090595; 8332577; 7184891}. Some rare cases may be iatrogenic {20068330}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T14:11:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62829,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>MED12</em> mutations have been demonstrated, but the mutation status in individual peritoneal nodules and concurrent uterine leiomyomas may be different {23635816}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62830,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are multiple, granular, white or tan miliary nodules, usually &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;cm or rarely much larger.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62831,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The nodules resemble typical or cellular leiomyomas. The smooth muscle cells are arranged in fascicles with absent to mild nuclear atypia and low mitotic activity {7184891; 7360455}. Rarely, epithelioid differentiation is encountered {1398545}. In pregnant patients, decidual cells may be prominent. Coexisting endometriosis or endosalpingiosis may be found in 10% of cases {12090595; 15875729}. Peritoneal tumours that have bland cytology and an increased number of mitoses but do not fulfil the criteria for leiomyosarcoma have been considered low-grade smooth muscle tumours of probable multicentric origin {23018207}. The tumours are positive for myogenic markers.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62832,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62833,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62834,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> miliary nodules of benign smooth muscle.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positivity for markers of smooth muscle differentiation.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-21T18:38:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62835,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62836,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 147,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are typically self-limiting, but if incompletely excised they may recur in a subsequent pregnancy {7184891}. Successful treatment with GnRH agonists or aromatase inhibitors has been achieved {11576235; 18669776}. Rarely, malignant transformation occurs {15581996; 22335043}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:15:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]